\details{
Pipeline: \code{\link[=survival_target]{survival_target()}} (martingale residuals of a null Cox model) ->
\code{\link[=discretize_quantile]{discretize_quantile()}} of the target and each feature into \code{q} equal-frequency
bins -> \code{delta} from \code{\link[=delta_permutation]{delta_permutation()}} (unless a fixed \code{delta} is supplied,
the inner-CV-grid alternative) -> \code{\link[=fcbf_select]{fcbf_select()}}. Only the permutation null
uses random numbers, so \code{seed} makes the whole call reproducible; with a fixed
\code{delta} it is deterministic.

Supplying \code{top} switches to the explicit-cardinality mode: the relevance/redundancy
machinery is unchanged (SU relevance, SU Markov-blanket pruning), but the set size
is capped at \code{top} predominant features instead of being decided by \code{delta}, and the
permutation null is skipped (so the call is deterministic without a \code{seed}). This is
the mode for the low-event / high-dimensional genomic regime, where the
permutation-null \code{delta} inflates above the signal and self-selection collapses
(\code{q = 2} is the validated choice there). It keeps the FCBF identity; only the
cardinality control changes.

Coarse discretisation makes many features share the same relevance (at \code{q = 2}
SU takes only a few dozen distinct values over thousands of genes). By default
those ties are broken by the absolute Spearman correlation of each continuous
feature with the pseudo-target, computed on the same training data, so the
selection does not depend on the column order of \code{X}. \code{tiebreak = "index"}
restores the classic column-order rule of the original implementation.
